{"id":"adult-day-attendance","title":"Adult day health centres: attendance against licensed capacity","question":"Do San Francisco's adult day health centres, which are paid per person per day, report more people attending than the building is licensed to hold?","finding":"Since in-person rules returned in October 2022, San Francisco centres reported average daily attendance above licensed capacity in 1 of 542 centre-months. Before that, while services were delivered remotely, they did so in 41 months, in line with centres statewide. In 2026 the middle San Francisco centre ran at 69% of capacity against 69% statewide.","how":"For every centre and shift in the State Department of Aging's monthly centre file (January 2020 to September 2026), average daily attendance was divided by the licensed capacity printed in the same row. The file's own \"utilization\" column switches between fractions and percentages, so it was not used. \"People on the rolls\" is the month's count of participants served, divided by capacity.","limits":"From spring 2020 to autumn 2022 the State let centres serve people at home or by phone, so attendance above the building's capacity in those months is expected and was common across California. More people can be on the rolls than fit in the building because few attend every day. The file gives no dollar figures; these centres are paid through Medi-Cal health plans, not by the City.","tier":"CLEARED","columns":[{"k":"centre","t":"Centre","type":"text"},{"k":"operator","t":"City-paid operator","type":"org"},{"k":"capacity","t":"Licensed capacity","type":"int"},{"k":"attendance","t":"Average daily attendance, last 12 months","type":"int"},{"k":"use","t":"Share of capacity","type":"pct"},{"k":"monthsOverSince","t":"Months over capacity since Oct 2022","type":"int"},{"k":"monthsOverRemote","t":"Months over capacity, remote period","type":"int"},{"k":"peakOnRolls","t":"Peak people on the rolls, times capacity","type":"times"},{"k":"biggestJump","t":"B